WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.749 --> 00:00:03.487
♪ 'Cause you make my earth quake ♪

00:00:03.487 --> 00:00:05.835
♪ Oh, you make my earth quake ♪

00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:09.560
That was a little taste of "Earfquake,"

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:11.476
a standout track from "Igor,"

00:00:11.476 --> 00:00:13.942
Tyler, the Creator's 2019 album

00:00:13.942 --> 00:00:17.020
that landed him the Grammys'
biggest hip-hop prize.

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:18.960
Issa Rae: And the Grammy goes to...

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:22.018
LA, stand up! "Igor," Tyler, the Creator.

00:00:23.290 --> 00:00:24.960
Narrator: And the highest critical acclaim

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:27.210
of any of his projects yet.

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:33.510
To understand what makes "Igor" special,

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:35.200
you just have to peel back the layers

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:37.040
on a song like "Earfquake."

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:39.340
It's the highest-charting
single off the album

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:41.260
and of Tyler's whole career.

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:44.335
And one of the reasons it's
so addicting to listen to

00:00:44.335 --> 00:00:47.280
is its intricately layered sound.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:50.300
At any given second of the
song, you've got a kaleidoscope

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:52.610
of different sounds whirling together.

00:00:52.610 --> 00:00:55.250
From the multipart vocal harmonies...

00:00:55.250 --> 00:01:00.100
♪ Don't leave, it's my fault ♪

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:02.218
to swelling synthesizers...

00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:11.493
with everything over a
rumbling, distorted bass.

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:18.490
Tyler writes and produces his own stuff,

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:20.042
so he deserves full credit

00:01:20.042 --> 00:01:22.920
for song arrangements like this one.

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:24.870
But making all those layers of sounds

00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:27.948
mesh together perfectly
required the handiwork

00:01:27.948 --> 00:01:31.571
of Tyler's go-to mixing
engineer, Neal Pogue.

00:01:31.571 --> 00:01:33.250
We visited Neal at his studio

00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:35.670
to find out how he helped
bring Tyler's song craft

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:37.114
to life on "Earfquake."

00:01:37.114 --> 00:01:40.722
♪ Crashing down, I'll need you ♪

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:44.970
Neal is a Grammy-winning audio mixer

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:46.183
who's worked with artists like

00:01:46.183 --> 00:01:50.700
OutKast, Lil Wayne, TLC, and Nicki Minaj.

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:52.145
And he's been at the mixing board

00:01:52.145 --> 00:01:54.490
for Tyler's last three projects,

00:01:54.490 --> 00:01:58.406
including 2017's critically
acclaimed "Flower Boy."

00:01:58.406 --> 00:02:00.249
Tyler: And it came out cool.

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:03.445
Narrator: With complex
musical arrangements like

00:02:03.445 --> 00:02:06.870
"Earfquake," mixing can
really make or break the song.

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:08.705
A mixer's job is to blend together

00:02:08.705 --> 00:02:10.870
all the components of a recording,

00:02:10.870 --> 00:02:13.890
drums, bass, melody, vocals, etc.,

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:15.548
into a finished product that sounds

00:02:15.548 --> 00:02:17.560
harmonious and beautiful.

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:20.504
Essentially, the mixer is
like an orchestra conductor,

00:02:20.504 --> 00:02:22.390
making sure all the
different sound sources

00:02:22.390 --> 00:02:25.940
mesh together as perfectly as possible.

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:27.907
Neal Pogue: So, how I build a mix is

00:02:27.907 --> 00:02:29.960
I always start with the drums first.

00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:31.640
Because, to me, drums are the

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:33.705
essential component to a song.

00:02:33.705 --> 00:02:38.705
♪ Make my earth quake ♪

00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:45.122
♪ Make my earth quake ♪

00:02:45.122 --> 00:02:46.352
♪ Oh, you make my earth ♪

00:02:46.352 --> 00:02:49.194
Neal: The next sound that's
very important is the bass.

00:02:49.194 --> 00:02:51.451
Narrator: It goes without
saying that a song called

00:02:51.451 --> 00:02:53.585
"Earfquake" needed the kind of bass that

00:02:53.585 --> 00:02:55.748
literally makes the ground shake.

00:02:55.748 --> 00:02:59.111
Neal: Boom, boom, boom.
It's long. [laughs]

00:02:59.111 --> 00:03:00.560
And big, you know what I mean.

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:03.040
Even though he's singing
and it has a melody,

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:05.420
he still wanted that bass to come through.

00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:07.710
Narrator: As a mixer,
Neal's pretty much an expert

00:03:07.710 --> 00:03:10.252
in the science of an amazing bass drop.

00:03:10.252 --> 00:03:11.735
♪ Oh, you make my earth... ♪

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:18.786
See, a lot of it has to do

00:03:18.786 --> 00:03:20.810
with sound frequencies.

00:03:20.810 --> 00:03:23.370
Basically, your ears
can detect a huge range

00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:26.960
of frequencies, from 20 hertz to 20,000,

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:28.590
and each element in a mix,

00:03:28.590 --> 00:03:30.872
whether it's bass, drums, or vocals,

00:03:30.872 --> 00:03:33.740
has energy in different
parts of this range.

00:03:33.740 --> 00:03:36.073
Mixers use a tool called EQ,

00:03:36.073 --> 00:03:37.550
or equalization,

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:40.780
to adjust the balance of
different frequencies in a mix,

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:44.170
filtering out some frequencies
while boosting others.

00:03:44.170 --> 00:03:47.499
Neal: Said EQ can make
you cry, dance, fight,

00:03:47.499 --> 00:03:49.541
you know, love, everything.

00:03:49.541 --> 00:03:54.453
Depending on the song,
which the frequency changes

00:03:54.453 --> 00:03:57.050
the color of the track.

00:03:57.050 --> 00:04:00.591
Narrator: Next came the
colorful part: the keys.

00:04:01.890 --> 00:04:04.712
Neal: Meaning the drums and
the bass, that's the groove.

00:04:04.712 --> 00:04:08.418
Stuff like keyboards and
pianos, that's the beauty.

00:04:15.410 --> 00:04:17.240
Narrator: Anyone familiar
with Tyler's music

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:19.290
knows that he loves his chords,

00:04:19.290 --> 00:04:21.240
and "Earfquake" is no exception.

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:23.950
The key riffs drive the
melodies in the song,

00:04:23.950 --> 00:04:25.920
and the piano's actually
the main instrument

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:27.780
you hear for a good part of the song

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:29.793
during Playboi Carti's verse.

00:04:29.793 --> 00:04:31.109
♪ Oh, my God, hold up ♪

00:04:31.109 --> 00:04:32.709
♪ These diamonds not Tiffany's ♪

00:04:32.709 --> 00:04:35.733
♪ So in love ♪

00:04:35.733 --> 00:04:36.991
♪ So in love ♪

00:04:36.991 --> 00:04:38.810
Neal: Tyler, being a keyboard player

00:04:38.810 --> 00:04:40.797
first and foremostly,

00:04:40.797 --> 00:04:44.230
it's all about keyboard
lines and keyboard chords,

00:04:44.230 --> 00:04:47.200
and that's a big part of him.

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:48.820
Narrator: Since it's Tyler, though,

00:04:48.820 --> 00:04:52.196
you need synth keys on
top of your piano keys.

00:04:52.196 --> 00:04:57.196
♪ Oh, you make my earth... ♪

00:05:01.999 --> 00:05:03.400
♪ Make my earth quake ♪

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:06.130
The focus here was on
making the synths and pianos

00:05:06.130 --> 00:05:09.920
sound sunny and buoyant, even
on top of those heavy drums.

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:11.170
Neal: Tyler loves colors.

00:05:11.170 --> 00:05:14.529
He loves, like, beautiful
bridges and things like that,

00:05:14.529 --> 00:05:17.100
so he loves for it to be colorful.

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:19.900
All the songs, he wanted
them to be brighter.

00:05:19.900 --> 00:05:22.910
You know, to really have that dynamic

00:05:22.910 --> 00:05:25.488
and to be right, coming right at you.

00:05:25.488 --> 00:05:28.050
Narrator: EQ can brighten
the whole tone of a song,

00:05:28.050 --> 00:05:30.460
helping instruments like
pianos and keyboards

00:05:30.460 --> 00:05:34.050
cut through what otherwise
might be a crowded mix.

00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:36.109
Neal: Now, when I put the EQs in...

00:05:38.572 --> 00:05:39.897
see? How...

00:05:41.172 --> 00:05:42.346
see how it brightens it up?

00:05:42.346 --> 00:05:43.505
Producer: Yeah.

00:05:43.505 --> 00:05:45.329
Neal: Now, if I take that brightness off,

00:05:45.329 --> 00:05:46.812
it's dull, see?

00:05:46.812 --> 00:05:48.670
It doesn't affect you the same way.

00:05:48.670 --> 00:05:50.830
Narrator: Besides playing
with the frequencies,

00:05:50.830 --> 00:05:52.440
one of the main ways you can make

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:54.616
a densely layered mix like "Earfquake"

00:05:54.616 --> 00:05:57.040
sound dynamic is with panning.

00:05:57.040 --> 00:06:00.110
Stereo panning is all
about how the song travels,

00:06:00.110 --> 00:06:01.645
with different sounds sweeping

00:06:01.645 --> 00:06:03.835
from left to right across the speakers

00:06:03.835 --> 00:06:06.842
or jumping from one of your
headphones to the other.

00:06:06.842 --> 00:06:08.756
Neal: From left to right.

00:06:08.756 --> 00:06:10.280
It's over there,

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:12.370
and now it's over here.

00:06:12.370 --> 00:06:14.242
Narrator: When all these
sounds are hitting your ears

00:06:14.242 --> 00:06:16.080
at different positions in time,

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:19.870
it makes for a much more
immersive listening experience.

00:06:19.870 --> 00:06:21.660
Like at the end of the album opener,

00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:23.643
"Igor's Theme," when panning keys

00:06:23.643 --> 00:06:26.243
float you right into the
beginning of "Earfquake."

00:06:30.292 --> 00:06:32.205
♪ For real, for real this time. ♪

00:06:32.205 --> 00:06:34.260
And then, once you hit the chorus,

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:36.637
the skipping synths almost make you feel

00:06:36.637 --> 00:06:38.041
like you're levitating.

00:06:38.041 --> 00:06:39.999
♪ You make my earth quake ♪

00:06:39.999 --> 00:06:43.537
♪ Oh, you make my earth quake ♪

00:06:43.537 --> 00:06:46.023
♪ Riding around, your
love is shaking me up ♪

00:06:46.023 --> 00:06:47.860
♪ And it's making my heart break ♪

00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:49.970
Neal: I call it headphone candy.

00:06:49.970 --> 00:06:51.580
There are a lot of things,
if you listen to the album,

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:53.980
you'll feel things panning,
like [makes noise],

00:06:53.980 --> 00:06:57.553
or coming across, taking you on a ride,

00:06:57.553 --> 00:06:59.480
creating your own little video

00:06:59.480 --> 00:07:01.767
inside your head of what that is.

00:07:01.767 --> 00:07:04.754
Narrator: The final track
Neal added was the vocals.

00:07:04.754 --> 00:07:07.668
♪ Make my earth quake ♪

00:07:07.668 --> 00:07:10.012
♪ Riding around, your
love is shaking me up ♪

00:07:10.012 --> 00:07:12.510
♪ And it's making my heart break ♪

00:07:12.510 --> 00:07:14.840
You've got ad-libs from Playboi Carti,

00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:17.120
backing vocals from Jessy Wilson,

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:20.830
an uncredited feature from
Dev Hynes, aka Blood Orange,

00:07:20.830 --> 00:07:24.900
and finally R&amp;B legend
Charlie Wilson on the chorus.

00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:26.630
One of the highlights of the track,

00:07:26.630 --> 00:07:28.900
and Tyler's personal favorite moment,

00:07:28.900 --> 00:07:31.440
comes from the melting
harmonies in the chorus.

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:33.590
Fine-tuning the balance
between those harmonies

00:07:33.590 --> 00:07:35.746
was part of the mixing process.

00:07:36.721 --> 00:07:39.754
♪ It's my fault ♪

00:07:39.754 --> 00:07:41.751
Neal: If you have a bunch of harmonies,

00:07:41.751 --> 00:07:43.830
you know, low harmony,
high harmony, whatever,

00:07:43.830 --> 00:07:46.640
I think, like, making sure
that the high harmony's

00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:48.280
not too louder than the low harmony,

00:07:48.280 --> 00:07:51.650
and, you know, then that
has to do with mixing.

00:07:51.650 --> 00:07:54.540
Narrator: Tyler's own vocals
are heavily manipulated

00:07:54.540 --> 00:07:56.608
so that he sounds near unrecognizable

00:07:56.608 --> 00:07:59.149
for much of the song up until the outro.

00:07:59.149 --> 00:08:01.503
♪ Complication, no ♪

00:08:01.503 --> 00:08:04.278
♪ I don't want no sovereign nation ♪

00:08:04.278 --> 00:08:08.256
♪ Crashing down, I'll need you ♪

00:08:08.256 --> 00:08:10.987
♪ 'Cause you make my earth quake ♪

00:08:10.987 --> 00:08:14.490
♪ Oh, you make my earth quake ♪

00:08:14.490 --> 00:08:16.680
♪ Riding around, you tell me something ♪

00:08:16.680 --> 00:08:18.710
Neal: Things had to be
pitched a certain way.

00:08:18.710 --> 00:08:21.390
A lot of times, he sent the vocals, like,

00:08:21.390 --> 00:08:25.070
to me that way, because that
was part of his production.

00:08:25.070 --> 00:08:26.580
Narrator: By layering digital effects

00:08:26.580 --> 00:08:28.690
on his usually distinctive voice,

00:08:28.690 --> 00:08:31.640
Tyler tucked his vocals
deeper into the mix

00:08:31.640 --> 00:08:34.044
on "Earfquake" and on the
rest of the album too.

00:08:34.044 --> 00:08:36.120
♪ Falling in love, yeah ♪

00:08:36.120 --> 00:08:38.430
Neal: If you notice, his
vocals are not very loud

00:08:38.430 --> 00:08:41.310
on this album, and that's on purpose.

00:08:41.310 --> 00:08:43.550
He want people to feel the music,

00:08:43.550 --> 00:08:45.630
but kind of lean in and
hear what he's saying

00:08:45.630 --> 00:08:48.533
rather than just be right in your face.

00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:51.870
Narrator: With panning,
Neal could space out

00:08:51.870 --> 00:08:53.106
the different vocal tracks

00:08:53.106 --> 00:08:55.330
along with the other
instruments in the mix,

00:08:55.330 --> 00:08:57.983
establishing a sense of
depth in the sound design.

00:08:59.210 --> 00:09:00.590
Neal: Everything can't be up front.

00:09:00.590 --> 00:09:02.590
Something has to have a backseat.

00:09:02.590 --> 00:09:04.753
So, it's kinda like looking through...

00:09:04.753 --> 00:09:06.160
it's like putting your
hands through something

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:09.530
and seeing that, it's like
layers. That's how I see it.

00:09:09.530 --> 00:09:11.890
It's like layers of
something on the left side,

00:09:11.890 --> 00:09:13.410
on the right side,
something's in the back,

00:09:13.410 --> 00:09:16.803
something's in the front,
something's panning across.

00:09:16.803 --> 00:09:19.020
Narrator: That's the difference
between a cluttered mix

00:09:19.020 --> 00:09:21.760
and a full, multidimensional one.

00:09:21.760 --> 00:09:23.450
Without mixing, some of the most

00:09:23.450 --> 00:09:25.700
beautifully chaotic moments on "Earfquake"

00:09:25.700 --> 00:09:28.160
wouldn't have had the same effect.

00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:30.090
One of the richest of these moments

00:09:30.090 --> 00:09:32.761
is the bridge that leads
into the song's outro.

00:09:32.761 --> 00:09:37.635
♪ Don't leave, it's my fault ♪

00:09:37.635 --> 00:09:39.860
♪ I said, don't leave ♪

00:09:39.860 --> 00:09:41.820
Neal: When it goes to
that little synth line,

00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:43.783
that part always captured
me, when it was out

00:09:43.783 --> 00:09:45.700
and everything is happening
during that moment.

00:09:45.700 --> 00:09:47.379
To have that cord progression

00:09:47.379 --> 00:09:50.135
and that synth line going through it

00:09:50.135 --> 00:09:52.070
and the melody of the background vocals,

00:09:52.070 --> 00:09:53.400
I was like, wow.

00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:54.816
To me, that's

00:09:54.816 --> 00:09:57.308
one of the most amazing
things he's ever done.

00:09:57.308 --> 00:10:01.149
♪ Oh, don't leave ♪

00:10:01.149 --> 00:10:04.417
♪ It's my fault ♪

00:10:04.417 --> 00:10:07.225
♪ Don't leave ♪

00:10:07.225 --> 00:10:10.041
♪ It's my fault ♪

00:10:10.041 --> 00:10:14.886
♪ 'Cause when it all comes
crashing down, I'll need you ♪

